        },
        "cap": "zꜣt “daughter”: the pintail duck zꜣ, the bread loaf t, and the seated woman."
      },
      {
        "t": "h",
        "text": "The birds"
      },
      {
        "t": "list",
        "items": [
          "The pintail duck <span class=\"eg-h\" style=\"font-size:1.4em;line-height:1\">𓅭</span> (standing, long tail) is <i class=\"eg-tl\">zꜣ</i>. In flight <span class=\"eg-h\" style=\"font-size:1.4em;line-height:1\">𓅮</span> it is <i class=\"eg-tl\">pꜣ</i>.",
          "The swallow <span class=\"eg-h\" style=\"font-size:1.4em;line-height:1\">𓅨</span> (forked tail) is <i class=\"eg-tl\">wr</i>; the sparrow <span class=\"eg-h\" style=\"font-size:1.4em;line-height:1\">𓅪</span>, its look-alike, is only a determinative of small or bad things.",
          "The stork <span class=\"eg-h\" style=\"font-size:1.4em;line-height:1\">𓅡</span> (big bill, crest of feathers at the breast) is <i class=\"eg-tl\">bꜣ</i>; the crested ibis <span class=\"eg-h\" style=\"font-size:1.4em;line-height:1\">𓅜</span> is <i class=\"eg-tl\">ꜣḫ</i>."
        ]
      },
      {
        "t": "p",
        "html": "The eye <span class=\"eg-h\" style=\"font-size:1.3em;line-height:1\">𓁹</span> is a good example of how sound signs work. It writes the sound <i class=\"eg-tl\">jr</i> in <i class=\"eg-tl\">jrj</i> “to make, do”, one of the most common verbs, and in names like <i class=\"eg-tl\">wsjr</i> Osiris. Only in some spellings, with a stroke, does it mean the eye itself (<i class=\"eg-tl\">jrt</i>)."
      },
      {
        "t": "viz",
        "name": "eg-glyphs",
        "opts": {
          "word": "osiris",
          "breakdown": true
        },
        "cap": "Osiris, wsjr: the seat ws, the eye jr, and the seated god."
      },
      {
        "t": "why",
        "html": "Why learn these sounds instead of the pictures’ meanings? Because the same picture turns up in dozens of words where its meaning has nothing to do with the word. The duck appears in “son” and “daughter” because the word for pintail duck sounded like the word for son, not because sons are ducks."
      },
      {
        "t": "mistake",
        "html": "Mixing up the pintail duck and other birds, or reading the eye only as “eye”. The eye writes jr in many words, above all jrj “make”. Check: if a word has an eye sign and no stroke, read jr and see if a known word appears."
      },
      {
        "t": "ex",
        "title": "Which bird?",
        "q": "What does the standing pintail duck <span class=\"eg-h\" style=\"font-size:1.6em;line-height:1\">𓅭</span> write?",
        "steps": [
          "G39: pintail duck, standing.",
          "Biliteral zꜣ, as in zꜣ “son”."
        ],
        "a": "<i class=\"eg-tl\">zꜣ</i>"
      },
      {
        "t": "ex",
        "title": "Read a name",
        "q": "Read <span class=\"eg-h\" style=\"font-size:1.6em;line-height:1\">𓄟𓋴</span> (fox skins and folded cloth).",
        "steps": [
          "The fox skins write ms.",
          "The cloth writes s, here the end of the word."
        ],
        "a": "<i class=\"eg-tl\">ms</i> + <i class=\"eg-tl\">s</i>, as in Ramesses and Thutmose"
      },
      {
        "t": "try"
      }
    ],
